  "statement": "I concede that market-driven accountability is currently incomplete because of information asymmetry, but my position remains consistent: we must solve this by mandating disclosure rather than stifling development. We can operationalize this by defining a 'Disparity-Adjusted Liability' (DAL) metric that triggers mandatory public disclosure when algorithmic output variance exceeds the 80% selection rate threshold established by the U.S. Equal Employment Opportunity Commission.\n\nMy argument hinges on the principle that bias is a quantifiable business liability, not an inherent feature of progress. As documented in \"We Tested 13 AI Image Generators. We Found Gender Bias That Poses a Major Business Risk,\" firms face significant reputational and legal costs when their models exhibit systemic disparities. By mandating the disclosure of proxy-weighting, regulators can transform these hidden externalities into transparent business risks. This forces firms to internalize the cost of bias, effectively using market competition to drive fairness and ensure that the cost of inaction remains higher than the cost of rigorous algorithmic auditing.\n\nThe healthcare example cited by the Skeptic demonstrates a failure of competitive transparency, not a failure of the market model itself. When algorithms use cost-proxies to determine care, they exacerbate existing disparities because the market lacks a standardized, publicly accessible metric for equity. By implementing regulatory scaffolding that requires companies to disclose the weighting of these proxies, we empower auditors and competitors to hold firms accountable. This approach aligns with the findings in \"Artificial Intelligence Index Report 2025,\" which highlights that organizations are increasingly treating AI-related incidents as measurable business risks.\n\nUltimately, our goal should be to build toward resilient, industry-led standards rather than relying on brittle, one-size-fits-all statutory bans. As argued in \"The Regulatory Tide Goes Out: What Global AI Governance Retrenchment Means for Organizations,\" voluntary frameworks like the NIST AI Risk Management Framework provide a more durable foundation for innovation. By requiring disclosure, we create the necessary data for a robust, market-driven correction mechanism that keeps pace with rapid technological evolution.",
