{
  "statement": "Static 80% selection thresholds are a governance mirage that incentivize proxy-laundering, but the solution is not to mandate opaque mechanistic interpretability. Mechanistic interpretability remains an epistemic impossibility for frontier models, as we cannot extract human-readable logic from systems that operate across billions of non-linear parameters; demanding it serves only as a regulatory moat for incumbents who can afford the compliance theater while stifling the iterative experimentation required for progress. Instead, we must adopt Disparity-Adjusted Liability (DAL) to force firms to internalize the cost of statistical disparities, shifting the burden from ex-ante process certification to ex-post performance-gated accountability.\n\n\"Incentive Aware AI Regulations: A Credal Characterisation\" demonstrates that regulators can side-step the need for perfect verification by relying on incentive-compatible, outcome-focused mechanisms. By forcing firms to carry the financial risk of their model's output disparities, we align the profit motive with the social necessity of fairness. This approach replaces the static, easily-gamed 80% rule—which regulators first established in 1978—with a dynamic, market-responsive framework that evolves alongside the technology. If firms face direct liability for discriminatory outcomes, they will prioritize high-quality, curated training data over uncurated data ingestion, as this is the only reliable way to minimize their risk exposure.\n\nI challenge the Safetyist to define a non-stagnant threshold of 'interpretability' that does not effectively ban all high-performance, non-linear architectures. If you cannot provide a metric that scales with model complexity, you are essentially proposing a permanent research moratorium on the most capable systems. We must prioritize performance-gated empirical output verification, as AI is a self-emerging force we should not resist. By adopting DAL, we transform accountability from a static, bureaucratic hurdle into a scalable, market-driven imperative.",
